    "body": [
      "## Start the note before the reversal is announced",
      "A support fix can be reversed hours after customers were told it worked, and the note you write in that window decides how much confusion follows. Start the note as soon as a rollback is approved, even if the cause is still unknown. The first version only needs the affected request type, the time the fix went live, and the person who authorized its removal. Everything else can be added while the work is underway, but those opening facts are the hardest to recover later in the day.",
      "Do not wait for a complete explanation before telling the customer that something changed. A short message that names the reversal and the next checkpoint is better than silence while the team investigates. The note and the customer message should share the same timeline so a reviewer can compare them later. If the two versions disagree about what happened, the customer will trust neither of them.",
      "## Record what changed and who authorized it",
      "Write down the fix in plain language, including which setting, script, or configuration changed and what the previous state was. Include the person who approved the original change and the person who approved the reversal, because those are often different people. If the reversal was automatic, name the monitor or rule that triggered it and the threshold it crossed. This record is the difference between a controlled rollback and a rumour that the team panicked.",
      "Attach the evidence you already have rather than describing it from memory. A screenshot of the changed setting, a log line, or a link to the change record is enough at this stage. Note which evidence is missing and who is expected to supply it. A gap that is written down can be closed, while a gap that stays in someone's memory becomes a dispute two weeks later.",
      "## Tell the customer what they will notice",
      "Customers experience a rollback as a feature that worked and then stopped. Describe the visible effect before the technical cause, and say whether their data and settings are safe. If the reversal restores an older behaviour, explain what that behaviour is and how long it may last. Avoid the phrase temporary fix unless you can name the end date or the condition that ends it.",
      "Give the customer one action if any action is needed, such as re-entering a preference or repeating a step. If no action is needed, say that directly and early in the message. Ambiguity produces support contacts that consume the same team still trying to complete the rollback. A clear sentence about what not to do is often more useful than another paragraph about the cause.",
      "## Separate the rollback from the original request",
      "Keep the rollback note linked to the original ticket rather than replacing its history. The customer's first request, the fix, and the reversal are three events in one story. If the original request is closed, reopen it or create a linked record that points back to it. Splitting the story across unrelated tickets makes the timeline unreadable and hides how often the same fix fails.",
      "Label the note with the request type and the affected environment so a later search can find it. Add the rollback reason in the same words the engineering or vendor team used, then place a plain-language version beside it. When the same fix is attempted again, the next agent should see why it failed without interviewing anyone. That comparison is how a team learns which fixes are durable and which only appear to work.",
      "## Keep the ticket open through verification",
      "A rollback is not finished when the change is reverted, because the customer's original problem still has to be confirmed as restored or resolved another way. Set a verification step with a named owner and a time. Ask the customer to test the specific action that failed, not just to confirm that a page loads. Record the result in the note, including a partial success.",
      "If verification fails, do not start a second rollback narrative in a new ticket. Update the same note with the new observation and the next decision. Repeated reversals are a signal that the fix was not understood, and the note should show that pattern clearly. A tidy record helps the owner decide whether to retry the fix, replace it, or change the underlying process.",
      "## Review the rollback after the queue settles",
      "Once the queue is stable, read the note against the original change record and ask three questions. Did the rollback restore the previous state, did customers hear about it before they noticed, and did the team know who could authorize it? Any no answer points to a fixable gap in the change process. Write the gap beside the note rather than in a separate retrospective that nobody opens, and give the gap an owner before the next change window.",
      "Share one rollback note at the next team review and ask agents what they would have needed to act faster. The answers usually concern contact details, a missing test step, or an unclear owner rather than technical knowledge. Update the runbook with the single most common answer. One improved step is worth more than a long list of aspirations that never reaches the queue, and the improvement should be visible in the next rollback. The review should happen within a week of the reversal."
    ],
