    "body": [
      "## Map the requests that depend on billing",
      "A billing cutover reaches the help desk long before the finance team announces a finish date. List every request type that reads from or writes to the billing system, including invoice copies, payment status, refunds, plan changes, and failed card retries. For each one, note the current answer, the system behind it, and the person who can confirm it after the change. This list is the scope of your support risk, and it should be shorter than the vendor's project plan.",
      "Mark the requests that cannot wait for the cutover to finish. A customer locked out of a payment method needs a path through the change window, even if the answer is a manual workaround with a clear limit. Requests that can wait should be tagged with a hold reason that the customer can see. The distinction prevents agents from promising a resolution date that the migration team cannot support.",
      "## Agree on a freeze window and its exceptions",
      "Write down the exact hours when normal billing changes stop, along with the requests that are still allowed. Freeze windows fail when they exist only in a project channel that the support team does not monitor. Publish the window in the queue tool, the shift notes, and the customer-facing status page. An agent should be able to answer whether a change is allowed without asking three people on two different teams.",
      "Name the exceptions before the window opens. Refunds above a threshold, failed autopay on a due date, and account closures usually need a named approver rather than a general instruction to use judgement. Record the approver's contact method and the expected response time. If the approver is unavailable, the exception list should show who takes over instead of leaving the queue to improvise.",
      "## Prepare answers for the questions customers will ask",
      "Customers rarely ask about the migration by name; they ask why a charge looks different or why a saved card no longer works. Draft short answers for the ten most likely questions, each with what is known, what is not known, and when the next update will come. Have the billing owner confirm every answer before the window opens. An unconfirmed script is a guess with a professional tone.",
      "Keep the answers in one place that agents can reach during a call without leaving the ticket. A knowledge article with a visible review date is better than a pinned message that scrolls away. Include the phrases agents should avoid, especially any promise about when a missing invoice will appear. Written boundaries protect both the customer and the agent when the cutover slips.",
      "## Protect the queue from silent duplicate work",
      "During a cutover, the same failed payment can arrive by email, chat, and a forwarded message from an account manager. Set a duplicate check that runs before an agent starts a billing investigation, using the customer, the amount, and the transaction date as keys. Link the records rather than deleting the second report. The customer may have sent it to a different team, and that context matters.",
      "Give one owner the job of watching for clusters rather than individual tickets. A pattern of identical failures usually means the cutover introduced a rule change rather than a set of unrelated customer mistakes. When the pattern appears, pause the script and check the system before more agents repeat the same explanation. Fast pattern recognition saves more time than faster typing.",
      "## Set a handoff for failed transactions",
      "Decide in advance which failures go to the billing team and which stay with support. A failed charge due to a declined card is a customer conversation, while a failed charge with no decline reason is a system question. Write the distinction as a short decision rule with examples. Agents should not have to guess whether a technical anomaly is theirs to explain.",
      "For every escalation, capture the customer, the amount, the timestamp, the error text, and the last successful transaction. Send that packet through one channel and record the reference in the ticket. A verbal handoff during a busy cutover is easily lost, and the customer will be asked for the same details twice. The packet is also the evidence the billing team needs to trace the failure.",
      "## Reopen normal routing in stages",
      "Do not restore every request type at the same moment. Start with read-only requests such as invoice copies, then move to low-risk changes, and leave payment reversals until the billing owner confirms reconciliation. Announce each stage to the queue with the time and the person who approved it. Staged reopening gives the team a chance to catch a bad rule before it touches every customer.",
      "Keep the old workaround visible for a short period after the new path opens. Agents who used the workaround for a week will return to it by habit, especially under pressure. Note the retirement date and ask a reviewer to sample tickets for the old method. Habits fade faster when someone checks than when a message says the change is live.",
      "## Review the first day after cutover",
      "On the first normal day, compare the billing request volume with the same weekday before the change. Look at the mix, not only the total, because a stable count can hide a shift from invoice questions to payment failures. Read the ten oldest billing tickets and check whether each has a current owner and a customer update. The oldest cases show whether the new routing works at the edges.",
      "Write a short cutover note with what changed, what broke, what the team worked around, and which script needs revision. Give it to the billing owner and the next shift lead. The note is more useful than a dashboard because it explains the exceptions behind the numbers. Update the knowledge article while the details are still fresh, then set a review date for the next billing cycle."
    ],
