    "title": "Decide what to do with a ticket that names the wrong product",
    "excerpt": "How to recover the real request when a customer labels a ticket with the wrong product, and how to stop the pattern.",
    "service": "helpdesk-operations",
    "minutes": 8,
    "published": "2026-11-18",
    "sources": [
      {"name": "BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: customer service representatives", "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/office-and-administrative-support/customer-service-representatives.htm"},
      {"name": "NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5 security and privacy controls", "url": "https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final"},
      {"name": "GSA customer experience guidance", "url": "https://www.gsa.gov/about-us/customer-experience"},
      {"name": "ISO 18295-1:2017 customer contact centres", "url": "https://www.iso.org/standard/70677.html"}
    ],
    "body": [
      "## Confirm the product before correcting the ticket",
      "A ticket that names the wrong product is usually a routing clue, not a customer error to be fixed. Start by confirming which product the customer can actually see, log into, or pay for. Ask for the screen name, the invoice line, or the email that brought them to support. Those details are more reliable than the product name in a dropdown.",
      "Do not change the product field until you understand the request. The label may be wrong because the customer picked the nearest option, because the form lists products in a confusing order, or because the customer uses two products from the same company. Each cause needs a different fix. Correcting the field first can hide the reason the request arrived in the wrong queue.",
      "## Ask what the customer was actually trying to do",
      "A product label describes a system, while the request describes a task. Ask what the customer was doing when the problem appeared and what they expected to happen next. A billing question filed under a reporting product may really be about a subscription that includes both. The task tells you which team can answer without another transfer, and it gives the ticket a purpose that survives a product rename.",
      "Repeat the task back in one sentence and ask the customer to confirm it. This small step catches misunderstandings before an agent spends time on the wrong system. It also gives the ticket a plain-language summary that survives a product rename. The summary should use the customer's words where possible and the team's category beside them, so the next agent can search by either one.",
      "## Route by the work, not the label",
      "Routing rules often match on the product field because it is easy to query. That design fails when the field is wrong or when one request spans two products. Add a secondary route based on the request type and the system named in the message. The first matching rule should not be the only chance to reach the right team.",
      "When two teams could own the work, choose the one that can answer the customer's actual question and ask it to coordinate the rest. A ticket that bounces between teams is worse than a ticket that starts in the less obvious place with a named owner. Record the routing decision so the next agent does not reopen the debate. The owner should be a queue, not a single person who might be on leave.",
      "Check whether the customer has more than one account before you move anything. A single login can hold access to several products, and the ticket may belong to the account rather than to the product named in the subject. Ask which account should be billed or supported, and note it in the ticket. Moving the wrong account creates a second problem while solving the first.",
      "## Update the taxonomy without breaking reports",
      "If the wrong product is a common choice, the intake form may need a clearer label or an extra question. Change the form only after you have evidence from several tickets. A single mislabeled request is a customer mistake, while a repeated pattern is a design problem. The distinction decides whether the fix belongs in training or in the form.",
      "Keep the old category available for historical reporting, even after you add a new one. Map the old value to the new value in the reporting layer rather than rewriting past tickets. Rewriting history makes trends look cleaner but destroys the evidence of how the queue actually behaved. A documented mapping is safer than a silent edit, and it lets a reviewer see both the old and the new view.",
      "## Tell the customer what changed",
      "If the ticket moves to another team, tell the customer where it went and what happens next. Do not describe the move as a correction of their mistake. Say that the request has been matched with the team that owns the product they described. This keeps the focus on progress rather than on who was wrong, and it makes the new owner easier to contact.",
      "Give a new reference if the destination system creates one, and keep the original visible in the history. A customer who has already sent screenshots should not be asked for them again. Confirm which attachments moved and which need to be re-sent before you close the first ticket. The handoff should reduce work for the customer, not repeat it under a different reference number.",
      "## Review mislabeled tickets for a pattern",
      "Sample the mislabeled tickets each month and group them by the product the customer chose and the product the work belonged to. A strong pair suggests a confusing label or a shared feature. Read a few examples to see whether the customer was guessing or following a prompt. The pattern is more useful than the count, because it points to the exact place in the form or the help article that needs to change.",
      "Fix the most common pair first and measure whether the mislabels fall. If they do not, the problem may be in the customer's mental model rather than the form. Add an example to the help center or change the order of the options. One clear improvement is enough for a month, and the queue will show whether it worked."
    ],
